.TH PTY_WAIT_EXIT 3 2026-10-03 "libptytest 0.1" "libptytest Manual"
.SH NAME
pty_wait_exit \- wait for the program under test to exit by itself
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <ptytest.h>
.PP
.BI "int pty_wait_exit(PtySession *" s ", int " timeout_ms );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR pty_wait_exit ()
waits for the child to exit on its own, reading and processing its output
meanwhile, so a child writing more than a PTY buffer holds cannot deadlock
against the wait.
Output it printed on its way out is on the screen when the call returns.
.PP
Use it for a program that finishes by itself \(em a one-shot command, a
TUI after its quit key \(em before inspecting what it wrote to disk.
.BR pty_close (3)
terminates a running child with
.B SIGTERM
and then
.BR SIGKILL ,
which can cut it off mid-write: the last screen line saying the work is
done is not a promise that the process has flushed its files and exited.
.PP
On success the child is reaped; a later
.BR pty_close (3)
does not signal it, and a second
.BR pty_wait_exit ()
returns \-1.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The child's exit status (0 to 255), or \-1 on timeout, when the child was
killed by a signal, or when there is no child to wait for.
Assert the status, not only the output: a program that prints the right
text and exits with 1 has failed.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
pty_send_str(s, "q");
ASSERT_EXIT_STATUS(s, 0, 3000);         /* see pty_assert(3) */
ASSERT_SCREEN_CONTAINS(s, "Saved 3 messages");
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pty_close (3),
.BR pty_signal (3),
.BR pty_assert (3)
